Subject: Open a local file URL without the OpenURI portal

QDesktopServices::openUrl() sends a local file that has a fragment or a
query, such as file:///usr/share/doc/HTML/en/kdos/04-programs/kdos-settings.html#kdos-look,
to the platform's openUrl() rather than openDocument(), so that the
fragment is kept, and openUrl() hands every URL to the desktop portal's
OpenURI while a portal is running. OpenURI refuses a file: URI, and its
answer comes on the request object after the call has returned success,
so Qt never falls back and nothing opens: not a Help button that names a
section of a local HTML handbook, and not Qt.openUrlExternally() on a
local page at an anchor.

With this change a local file outside a sandbox goes to the launcher,
xdg-open, with its fragment; in a Plasma session that runs kde-open,
which opens the page in the default browser at the section. Inside a
Flatpak or Snap sandbox, where no launcher reaches the host, it goes to
the portal's OpenFile, which takes the file without its fragment.

To be offered upstream against qt/qtbase.

--- a/src/gui/platform/unix/qdesktopunixservices.cpp
+++ b/src/gui/platform/unix/qdesktopunixservices.cpp
@@ -568,6 +568,18 @@
 
 bool QDesktopUnixServices::openUrl(const QUrl &url)
 {
+    // The portal's OpenURI refuses a file: URI and answers asynchronously, after the call has
+    // returned success, so a local file never goes there. In a sandbox it is opened through
+    // OpenFile, which takes the file without its fragment or query; outside one the launcher
+    // is given the whole URL, fragment included.
+    if (url.isLocalFile()) {
+#  if QT_CONFIG(dbus)
+        if (!m_hasNoPortal && checkNeedPortalSupport())
+            return runWithXdgActivationToken(&QDesktopUnixServices::openDocumentWithPortal, url);
+#  endif
+        return runWithXdgActivationToken(&QDesktopUnixServices::openUrlWithoutPortal, url);
+    }
+
     if (!m_hasNoPortal)
         return runWithXdgActivationToken(&QDesktopUnixServices::openUrlWithPortal, url);
 
